  <h2>Examples</h2>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`--Basic usage
local function main()
    local testList = {"one", "two", "three"}
    local length = #testList
    ll.OwnerSay("There are " .. tostring(length) .. " entries in the list.")
end

main()
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`//Basic usage
default
{
    state_entry()
    {
        list testList = ["one", "two", "three"];
        integer length = llGetListLength(testList);
        llOwnerSay("There are " + (string)length + " entries in the list.");
    }
}`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <h2>Notes</h2>
  <h3>Best Practices</h3>
  <p>When using list length to help you loop through a list, it is better to determine the length first, then start your loop:</p>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`integer length = llGetListLength(mylist);

integer index;// default is 0
while (index < length)
{
    llSay(0, llList2String(mylist, index));
    ++index;
}`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <p>The following example is to illustrate what not to do, it calculates the length in the "for" loop and is inefficient because the length gets recalculated at each pass through the loop. This should only ever be done if the list is in fact changing (in length) with each iteration of the loop[[1]](#footnote_1).</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`local index = 0  -- default is 0
`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`integer index;// default is 0
while (index < llGetListLength(mylist))
{
    llSay(0, llList2String(mylist, index));
    ++index;
}`} lang="lsl" /></div>
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td></td>
        <td><b>Important:</b> Please read <a href="https://wiki.secondlife.com/wiki/User:Kireji_Haiku/How_to_deal_with_lists_in_LSL" target="_blank">this intro of how to iterate over a list in LSL</a>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>LSO Optimizations</h3>
  <p>A faster and lighter (in bytecode) way to determine the length of a list is to do a not-equals compare with a null list. This works because the list not-equals compare returns the difference between the lengths, meaning that it returns the same result as `llGetListLength()`, minus the overhead in bytecode, and performance penalty of calling a non-native function. Note: This optimization is much less beneficial time-wise in Mono as Mono's llGetListLength function is almost twice as fast, however the bytecode saving is still about 30 bytes.</p>
<div class="lua-block"><span class="tag lua-tag">Lua</span><DocsCode code={`local in_t = {}
local len_in = #in_t
local flen_in = (#in_t)
--flen_in and len_in will be the same

local neg_len_in = -#in_t
local fneg_len_in = (0 - #in_t)
--fneg_len_in and neg_len_in will be the same`} lang="slua" /></div>
<div class="lsl-block"><span class="tag lsl-tag">LSL</span><DocsCode code={`list in;
integer len_in = llGetListLength(in);
integer flen_in = (in != []);
//flen_in and len_in will be the same

integer neg_len_in = -llGetListLength(in);
integer fneg_len_in = ([] != in);
//fneg_len_in and neg_len_in will be the same`} lang="lsl" /></div>
